  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q23-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "23",
    "prompt": "Sally is initially located at the origin, $O$. She then rolls a four-sided die. She moves north if she obtains a 1, east for a 2, south for a 3 and west for a 4. Find the probability that Sally can be found at the origin after:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q23-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "2 rolls",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac14$",
          "worked": "Write $N,E,S,W$ for the four directions. The return paths are $NS$, $SN$, $EW$ and $WE$. There are $4^2=16$ equally likely paths.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{return after 2 rolls})&=\\frac{4}{16}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{return after 2 rolls})&=\\frac14.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q23-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "3 rolls",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$0$",
          "worked": "Returning to the origin requires equal numbers of north and south moves and equal numbers of east and west moves. The total number of moves must therefore be even, so returning after 3 rolls is impossible.\n\n$$\\Pr(\\text{return after 3 rolls})=0.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q23-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "4 rolls.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{9}{64}$\n\nCount the arrangements of $NNSS$, $EEWW$ and $NESW$.",
          "worked": "The possible direction collections for a return are $NNSS$, $EEWW$ and $NESW$. Each of the first two has 6 arrangements: choose one repeated direction's two positions from $12,13,14,23,24,34$. The four distinct directions have $4\\times3\\times2\\times1=24$ arrangements. There are $4^4=256$ equally likely paths.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{return after 4 rolls})&=\\frac{6+6+24}{256}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{return after 4 rolls})&=\\frac{36}{256}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{return after 4 rolls})&=\\frac{9}{64}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      }
    ]
  },
